> > > If I change those static's in the patches (https://github.com/termux/termux-packages/tree/master/ndk-patches/29) the configure fails, but if I leave them as is the compilation fails.
> > 
> > 
> > the issue suggests that upstream android headers currently also contain such statics, could you confirm whether the Termux header patches contain the only code that needs to be edited in order for this to be successful, or does it also require editing unmodified parts of Android headers?
> 
> In a personal project that uses `import std` for it to compile on device on Termux I had to define `-D__BIONIC_CTYPE_INLINE=inline` and `sed -i "/__inline__/ s/static//" /data/data/com.termux/files/usr/include/stdio.h` to remove the statics. I don't know if doing that breaks other stuff.

I was able to do this in order to build your demo without editing the `stdio.h` in between configure and build:

```c
#ifndef __cplusplus 
#define _TERMUX_STATIC static
#else
#define _TERMUX_STATIC
#endif
```

```c
/* Used by perl, fish, and others. */
#if !defined(__swift__)
_TERMUX_STATIC __inline__ char* _Nonnull ctermid(char* _Nullable s) {
	if (s == 0) return (char*) "/dev/tty";
	strcpy(s, "/dev/tty");
	return s;
}
#endif
```

```c
_TERMUX_STATIC __inline__ FILE* _Nullable tmpfile() {
	int p = getpid();
	char* path;
	int i;
	for (i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
		unsigned int r = arc4random();
		if (asprintf(&path, "/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/tmp/tmpfile.%d-%u", p, r) == -1) return NULL;
		int fd = open(path, O_RDWR | O_CREAT | O_EXCL | O_LARGEFILE, 0600);
		if (fd >= 0) {
			FILE* result = fdopen(fd, "w+");
			unlink(path);
			free(path);
			return result;
		}
		free(path);
	}
	return NULL;
}
```

however, this is just a small experiment and I don't know if my idea affects other things also.

I believe that `static` has a important effect in C++ that is actually similar to its behavior in C, so having to remove it from the C++ version of the header could still have unexpected side effects, I think.

